Chuckle.

That's a nice answer for *nix play, but not a general
solution, while so far as I know, the MT is such a
solution for any platform where C compiles.

There is still the problem of providing a seed,
though, since the MT accepts a _lot_ of
initialization seeding:

     "The algorithm operates on a seed value which is
     19,937 bits long; this value is stored in an
     array with 624 elements which contain 32-bit
     values."
     http://www.quadibloc.com/crypto/co4814.htm

Though that's been overtaken by events since
that page was written, some versions of MT
now accept arbitrary seed sizes.

A commonly accepted(?) way to do the seeding

     "A small lagged Fibonacci generator or linear
     congruential generator gets started much quicker
     and usually is used to seed the Mersenne
     Twister."

is mentioned here:

     http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mersenne_Twister

There's more to read on the general issue of
seeding the MT than I care to review:

     http://www.google.com/search?q=mersenne+twister+seeding

It's also interesting to read from the Wikipedia
page that the MT is not in the least bit
cryptographically secure.

xanthian, using /dev/srandom and so making sure the
terrorists don't intercept my NetHack's RNG's
choices.




